================================================================
TOPIC 1 - SELECT - Get Data
================================================================
CONCEPT:
SELECT is used to get data from table. Like you ask - Give me data.

COMMAND:
SELECT column_name FROM table_name;

EXAMPLE CODE:
-- Get all columns, all rows
SELECT * FROM loan_table;

-- Get only name and cibil
SELECT name, cibil FROM loan_table;

OUTPUT:
id | name   | cibil | salary | city
1  | Hyarel | 750   | 80000  | Mumbai
2  | Priya  | 680   | 60000  | Delhi
3  | Rahul  | 550   | 40000  | Pune
4  | Sneha  | 720   | 90000  | Mumbai
5  | Amit   | 600   | 50000  | Delhi

PROJECT USE:
In FastAPI, when user opens dashboard, you use SELECT to get all loan customers from PostgreSQL.

INTERVIEW ANSWER:
SELECT is used to fetch data from table.

================================================================
TOPIC 2 - WHERE - Filter Data
================================================================
CONCEPT:
WHERE filters rows. Only rows that match condition will come.
Without WHERE, you get all rows. With WHERE, you get only useful rows.

COMMAND:
SELECT * FROM table WHERE condition;

EXAMPLE CODE:
-- Only CIBIL > 600
SELECT * FROM loan_table WHERE cibil > 600;

-- Only Mumbai customers
SELECT * FROM loan_table WHERE city = 'Mumbai';

OUTPUT for first query:
Hyarel 750 Mumbai
Priya  680 Delhi
Sneha  720 Mumbai
Amit   600 Delhi - No, 600 is not > 600, so Amit will NOT come. Only 3 rows.

PROJECT USE:
In RAG project, when user asks for high CIBIL customers, you use WHERE cibil > 600.

INTERVIEW ANSWER:
WHERE is used to filter rows based on condition.

================================================================
TOPIC 3 - AND, OR, NOT - Multiple Conditions
================================================================
CONCEPT:
AND = Both conditions must be true.
OR = Any one condition true is enough.
NOT = Opposite.

COMMAND:
SELECT * FROM table WHERE condition1 AND condition2;
SELECT * FROM table WHERE condition1 OR condition2;
SELECT * FROM table WHERE NOT condition;

EXAMPLE CODE:
-- AND - CIBIL > 600 AND city Mumbai - Both must be true
SELECT * FROM loan_table WHERE cibil > 600 AND city = 'Mumbai';

OUTPUT:
Hyarel 750 Mumbai
Sneha  720 Mumbai

-- OR - City Mumbai OR Delhi
SELECT * FROM loan_table WHERE city = 'Mumbai' OR city = 'Delhi';

OUTPUT:
Hyarel, Priya, Sneha, Amit - 4 rows, Rahul Pune will not come.

-- NOT - NOT Mumbai
SELECT * FROM loan_table WHERE NOT city = 'Mumbai';

OUTPUT:
Priya, Rahul, Amit

PROJECT USE:
Filter Mumbai customers with high CIBIL for campaign.

INTERVIEW ANSWER:
AND means both true, OR means any one true, NOT means opposite.

================================================================
TOPIC 4 - ORDER BY - Sort Data
================================================================
CONCEPT:
ORDER BY sorts data.
ASC = Ascending, Small to Big, A to Z, Default.
DESC = Descending, Big to Small, Z to A.

COMMAND:
SELECT * FROM table ORDER BY column ASC / DESC;

EXAMPLE CODE:
-- Highest CIBIL first
SELECT * FROM loan_table ORDER BY cibil DESC;

OUTPUT:
Hyarel 750
Sneha  720
Priya  680
Amit   600
Rahul  550

-- Lowest salary first
SELECT * FROM loan_table ORDER BY salary ASC;

PROJECT USE:
Show top customers first in dashboard.

INTERVIEW ANSWER:
ORDER BY sorts result. ASC is small to big, DESC is big to small.

================================================================
TOPIC 5 - LIMIT - Limit Rows
================================================================
CONCEPT:
LIMIT shows only few rows. When table has 1 Lakh rows, you don't want all rows. You want top 10.

COMMAND:
SELECT * FROM table ORDER BY column DESC LIMIT number;

EXAMPLE CODE:
-- Top 3 highest CIBIL
SELECT * FROM loan_table ORDER BY cibil DESC LIMIT 3;

OUTPUT:
Hyarel 750
Sneha  720
Priya  680

-- First 2 rows only
SELECT * FROM loan_table LIMIT 2;

PROJECT USE:
In FastAPI, show top 10 customers, not 1 Lakh customers.

INTERVIEW ANSWER:
LIMIT is used to limit number of rows.

================================================================
TOPIC 6 - DISTINCT - No Duplicate
================================================================
CONCEPT:
DISTINCT removes duplicate values. Shows only unique values.

COMMAND:
SELECT DISTINCT column FROM table;

EXAMPLE CODE:
-- Unique cities
SELECT DISTINCT city FROM loan_table;

OUTPUT:
Mumbai
Delhi
Pune

Without DISTINCT, city would be: Mumbai, Delhi, Pune, Mumbai, Delhi - 5 rows with duplicate.
With DISTINCT, only 3 unique cities.

PROJECT USE:
Get list of all cities where bank has customers, without duplicate.

INTERVIEW ANSWER:
DISTINCT removes duplicate values.

================================================================
TOPIC 7 - IN, BETWEEN, LIKE - Special Filter
================================================================
CONCEPT:
IN = Value is in list.
BETWEEN = Value between two values, including both.
LIKE = Pattern matching, % means anything, _ means one character.

COMMAND:
SELECT * FROM table WHERE column IN (value1, value2);
SELECT * FROM table WHERE column BETWEEN value1 AND value2;
SELECT * FROM table WHERE column LIKE 'pattern';

EXAMPLE CODE:
-- IN
SELECT * FROM loan_table WHERE city IN ('Mumbai', 'Delhi');

-- BETWEEN - CIBIL 600 to 750, including 600 and 750
SELECT * FROM loan_table WHERE cibil BETWEEN 600 AND 750;

OUTPUT:
Hyarel 750, Priya 680, Sneha 720, Amit 600 - 4 rows. Rahul 550 will not come.

-- LIKE - Name starts with H
SELECT * FROM loan_table WHERE name LIKE 'H%';

OUTPUT:
Hyarel

-- LIKE - Name contains 'a'
SELECT * FROM loan_table WHERE name LIKE '%a%';

OUTPUT:
Hyarel, Priya, Rahul, Sneha, Amit - All have a.

PROJECT USE:
IN for cities, BETWEEN for CIBIL range, LIKE for search name.

INTERVIEW ANSWER:
IN checks list, BETWEEN checks range, LIKE checks pattern.

================================================================
TOPIC 8 - Aggregate Functions - COUNT, SUM, AVG, MIN, MAX
================================================================
CONCEPT:
Aggregate = One answer from many rows.
COUNT = How many rows?
SUM = Total of column.
AVG = Average.
MIN = Smallest value.
MAX = Biggest value.

COMMAND:
SELECT COUNT(*) FROM table;
SELECT SUM(column) FROM table;
SELECT AVG(column) FROM table;
SELECT MIN(column) FROM table;
SELECT MAX(column) FROM table;

EXAMPLE CODE:
SELECT COUNT(*) FROM loan_table;
-- OUTPUT: 5

SELECT AVG(cibil) FROM loan_table;
-- OUTPUT: 660  (750+680+550+720+600 = 3300 / 5 = 660)

SELECT SUM(salary) FROM loan_table;
-- OUTPUT: 320000

SELECT MIN(cibil) FROM loan_table;
-- OUTPUT: 550

SELECT MAX(cibil) FROM loan_table;
-- OUTPUT: 750

PROJECT USE:
How many customers? Average CIBIL? Total salary? For dashboard and Matplotlib graph.

INTERVIEW ANSWER:
Aggregate functions give one result from many rows - COUNT, SUM, AVG, MIN, MAX.

================================================================
TOPIC 9 - GROUP BY - Group Data
================================================================
CONCEPT:
GROUP BY groups same values together, then you apply aggregate on each group.
Like Pandas groupby.

COMMAND:
SELECT column, COUNT(*) FROM table GROUP BY column;

EXAMPLE CODE:
-- How many customers in each city?
SELECT city, COUNT(*) FROM loan_table GROUP BY city;

OUTPUT:
Mumbai 2
Delhi  2
Pune   1

-- Average salary in each city
SELECT city, AVG(salary) FROM loan_table GROUP BY city;

OUTPUT:
Mumbai (80000+90000)/2 = 85000
Delhi  (60000+50000)/2 = 55000
Pune   40000

PROJECT USE:
Data Scientist uses GROUP BY to analyze - Which city has more customers?

INTERVIEW ANSWER:
GROUP BY groups same values and applies aggregate on each group.

================================================================
TOPIC 10 - HAVING - Filter After GROUP BY
================================================================
CONCEPT:
WHERE filters before grouping.
HAVING filters after grouping.
You cannot use WHERE with aggregate, you must use HAVING.

COMMAND:
SELECT column, COUNT(*) FROM table GROUP BY column HAVING COUNT(*) > number;

EXAMPLE CODE:
-- Only cities with more than 1 customer
SELECT city, COUNT(*) FROM loan_table GROUP BY city HAVING COUNT(*) > 1;

OUTPUT:
Mumbai 2
Delhi  2
Pune will NOT come because count is 1, not > 1.

-- Wrong way - This will give error:
-- SELECT city, COUNT(*) FROM loan_table GROUP BY city WHERE COUNT(*) > 1; -- WRONG

PROJECT USE:
Show only cities with more than 50 customers for campaign.

INTERVIEW ANSWER:
WHERE filters before GROUP BY, HAVING filters after GROUP BY.

================================================================
TOPIC 11 - JOIN - Combine Two Tables - MOST IMPORTANT
================================================================
CONCEPT:
JOIN combines two tables using common column.
Types:
INNER JOIN = Only matching in both tables.
LEFT JOIN = All from left table + matching from right. If no match, NULL.
RIGHT JOIN = All from right + matching from left.
FULL JOIN = All from both.

Create Second Table For Example:
CREATE TABLE bank_table (city VARCHAR(50), branch VARCHAR(50));
INSERT INTO bank_table VALUES ('Mumbai', 'Andheri Branch');
INSERT INTO bank_table VALUES ('Delhi', 'Connaught Branch');
INSERT INTO bank_table VALUES ('Chennai', 'T Nagar Branch');

COMMAND:
SELECT columns FROM table1 INNER JOIN table2 ON table1.column = table2.column;

EXAMPLE CODE:
-- INNER JOIN - Only cities present in both tables
SELECT loan_table.name, bank_table.branch 
FROM loan_table 
INNER JOIN bank_table ON loan_table.city = bank_table.city;

OUTPUT:
Hyarel - Andheri Branch (Mumbai)
Priya - Connaught Branch (Delhi)
Sneha - Andheri Branch (Mumbai)
Amit - Connaught Branch (Delhi)
Rahul will NOT come because Pune has no branch in bank_table.

-- LEFT JOIN - All loan customers, even if branch not found
SELECT loan_table.name, bank_table.branch 
FROM loan_table 
LEFT JOIN bank_table ON loan_table.city = bank_table.city;

OUTPUT:
Hyarel - Andheri Branch
Priya - Connaught Branch
Rahul - NULL (Pune has no branch)
Sneha - Andheri Branch
Amit - Connaught Branch

PROJECT USE:
Combine customer table + branch table to show customer + branch in dashboard.

INTERVIEW ANSWER:
JOIN combines two tables. INNER gives only matching, LEFT gives all left + matching right.

================================================================
TOPIC 12 - Subquery - Query Inside Query
================================================================
CONCEPT:
Subquery = One query inside another query. Inner query runs first, outer query uses its result.

COMMAND:
SELECT * FROM table WHERE column > (SELECT AVG(column) FROM table);

EXAMPLE CODE:
-- Customers with CIBIL more than average CIBIL (660)
SELECT * FROM loan_table WHERE cibil > (SELECT AVG(cibil) FROM loan_table);

Inner query: SELECT AVG(cibil) = 660
Outer query: SELECT * WHERE cibil > 660

OUTPUT:
Hyarel 750
Priya  680
Sneha  720

-- Customers in cities where branch exists
SELECT * FROM loan_table WHERE city IN (SELECT city FROM bank_table);

PROJECT USE:
Get customers above average, without calculating average manually.

INTERVIEW ANSWER:
Subquery is query inside query, inner runs first.

================================================================
TOPIC 13 - UNION - Combine Results
================================================================
CONCEPT:
UNION combines results of two SELECT queries.
UNION removes duplicate.
UNION ALL keeps duplicate.
Both SELECTs must have same number of columns.

COMMAND:
SELECT column FROM table WHERE condition
UNION
SELECT column FROM table WHERE condition;

EXAMPLE CODE:
SELECT name FROM loan_table WHERE city = 'Mumbai'
UNION
SELECT name FROM loan_table WHERE city = 'Delhi';

OUTPUT:
Hyarel
Sneha
Priya
Amit
No duplicate, 4 names.

-- UNION ALL - Keeps duplicate if any
SELECT city FROM loan_table
UNION ALL
SELECT city FROM bank_table;

PROJECT USE:
Combine Mumbai + Delhi customers for campaign.

INTERVIEW ANSWER:
UNION combines two SELECT results, removes duplicate. UNION ALL keeps duplicate.

================================================================
TOPIC 14 - INSERT - Add New Data
================================================================
CONCEPT:
INSERT adds new row to table.

COMMAND:
INSERT INTO table (column1, column2) VALUES (value1, value2);

EXAMPLE CODE:
INSERT INTO loan_table VALUES (6, 'Karan', 710, 75000, 'Pune');

-- With column names - Better way
INSERT INTO loan_table (id, name, cibil, salary, city) VALUES (6, 'Karan', 710, 75000, 'Pune');

-- Check
SELECT * FROM loan_table WHERE name = 'Karan';

OUTPUT:
6 Karan 710 75000 Pune

PROJECT USE:
When new user registers in FastAPI, backend does INSERT to PostgreSQL.

INTERVIEW ANSWER:
INSERT adds new row.

================================================================
TOPIC 15 - UPDATE - Change Data
================================================================
CONCEPT:
UPDATE changes existing data.
ALWAYS use WHERE, otherwise all rows will change. This is biggest mistake in interview and real job.

COMMAND:
UPDATE table SET column = new_value WHERE condition;

EXAMPLE CODE:
-- Update CIBIL of Hyarel from 750 to 760
UPDATE loan_table SET cibil = 760 WHERE name = 'Hyarel';

-- Check
SELECT * FROM loan_table WHERE name = 'Hyarel';

OUTPUT:
Hyarel 760

-- DANGER - Without WHERE, all rows change - NEVER DO THIS:
-- UPDATE loan_table SET cibil = 760; -- This will change all 5 customers to 760. Wrong.

PROJECT USE:
Update CIBIL when customer CIBIL increases.

INTERVIEW ANSWER:
UPDATE changes data, always use WHERE.

================================================================
TOPIC 16 - DELETE - Remove Data
================================================================
CONCEPT:
DELETE removes row.
ALWAYS use WHERE, otherwise all rows will be deleted.

COMMAND:
DELETE FROM table WHERE condition;

EXAMPLE CODE:
-- Delete Rahul
DELETE FROM loan_table WHERE name = 'Rahul';

-- Check
SELECT * FROM loan_table;

OUTPUT:
4 rows left, Rahul gone.

-- DANGER - Without WHERE, all data gone:
-- DELETE FROM loan_table; -- Deletes all rows. Never do without WHERE.

Difference DELETE vs DROP vs TRUNCATE:
DELETE = Deletes rows, table stays, you can rollback.
DROP = Deletes table fully, structure also gone.
TRUNCATE = Deletes all rows fast, table stays, cannot rollback easily.

PROJECT USE:
Delete customer who closed account.

INTERVIEW ANSWER:
DELETE removes row, always use WHERE.

================================================================
TOPIC 17 - CREATE TABLE, ALTER TABLE, DROP TABLE
================================================================
CONCEPT:
CREATE TABLE = Make new table.
ALTER TABLE = Change table structure - Add column, drop column, change type.
DROP TABLE = Delete table fully.

COMMAND:
CREATE TABLE table_name (column type, column type);
ALTER TABLE table_name ADD COLUMN column type;
ALTER TABLE table_name DROP COLUMN column;
DROP TABLE table_name;

EXAMPLE CODE:
-- CREATE
CREATE TABLE payment_table (
    payment_id INT PRIMARY KEY,
    loan_id INT,
    amount INT
);

-- ALTER - Add new column city
ALTER TABLE loan_table ADD COLUMN email VARCHAR(100);

-- ALTER - Drop column
ALTER TABLE loan_table DROP COLUMN email;

-- DROP - Delete payment_table fully
DROP TABLE payment_table;

PROJECT USE:
Create table for new project, add new column when requirement changes.

INTERVIEW ANSWER:
CREATE makes table, ALTER changes table, DROP deletes table.

================================================================
TOPIC 18 - Primary Key, Foreign Key
================================================================
CONCEPT:
Primary Key = Unique ID for each row. No duplicate, no NULL. Like Aadhar number. One table has one Primary Key.
Foreign Key = ID from other table. Links two tables. Creates relation.

COMMAND:
CREATE TABLE table (id INT PRIMARY KEY, ...);
CREATE TABLE table2 (id INT PRIMARY KEY, table1_id INT, FOREIGN KEY (table1_id) REFERENCES table1(id));

EXAMPLE CODE:
CREATE TABLE loan_table2 (
    id INT PRIMARY KEY,
    name VARCHAR(50)
);

CREATE TABLE payment_table2 (
    payment_id INT PRIMARY KEY,
    loan_id INT,
    amount INT,
    FOREIGN KEY (loan_id) REFERENCES loan_table2(id)
);

-- Insert
INSERT INTO loan_table2 VALUES (1, 'Hyarel');
INSERT INTO payment_table2 VALUES (101, 1, 5000); -- loan_id 1 exists, OK
-- INSERT INTO payment_table2 VALUES (102, 99, 5000); -- loan_id 99 does NOT exist, ERROR - Foreign Key protects.

PROJECT USE:
Loan table id links to Payment table. Ensures payment always belongs to real loan.

INTERVIEW ANSWER:
Primary Key is unique ID, Foreign Key links two tables.

================================================================
TOPIC 19 - Constraints - NOT NULL, UNIQUE, CHECK, DEFAULT
================================================================
CONCEPT:
Constraints = Rules for data. Database will reject wrong data.
NOT NULL = Must have value, cannot be empty.
UNIQUE = No duplicate value.
CHECK = Must follow condition.
DEFAULT = If no value given, use default value.

COMMAND:
CREATE TABLE table (column type NOT NULL, column type UNIQUE, column type CHECK(condition), column type DEFAULT value);

EXAMPLE CODE:
CREATE TABLE loan_table3 (
    id INT PRIMARY KEY,
    name VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL,
    email VARCHAR(100) UNIQUE,
    cibil INT CHECK (cibil > 300 AND cibil <= 900),
    city VARCHAR(50) DEFAULT 'Mumbai'
);

-- Test:
-- INSERT INTO loan_table3 VALUES (1, NULL, 'a@gmail.com', 750, 'Delhi'); -- ERROR, name NOT NULL
-- INSERT INTO loan_table3 VALUES (1, 'Hyarel', 'a@gmail.com', 200, 'Delhi'); -- ERROR, cibil CHECK fails, must be > 300
-- INSERT INTO loan_table3 (id, name, email, cibil) VALUES (1, 'Hyarel', 'a@gmail.com', 750); -- OK, city will be Mumbai by DEFAULT

PROJECT USE:
Ensure data is correct - CIBIL always between 300 and 900, email always unique.

INTERVIEW ANSWER:
Constraints are rules - NOT NULL must have value, UNIQUE no duplicate, CHECK must follow condition, DEFAULT gives default value.

